1. Directory Traversal is also called **Path Traversal**.
2. It occurs when user-controlled path information allows access outside the intended directory.
3. `..` commonly represents the parent directory.
4. Path normalization/canonicalization is important.
5. Security decisions should be made using the resolved/canonical path.
6. Blocking only `..` is not a complete defense.
7. URL encoding and decoding behavior must be considered.
8. Symbolic links can complicate path validation.
9. Windows and Linux have different filesystem semantics.
10. Prefer application-controlled file IDs over arbitrary filesystem paths.
11. Use allowlists where practical.
12. Apply least-privilege filesystem permissions.
13. Zip Slip is a related archive-extraction path traversal problem.
14. Detailed filesystem errors can cause information disclosure.
15. Directory Traversal does not automatically mean RCE.
16. File read and file write have different impacts.
